# Check-in chat

`CheckInButton` (`check-in.tsx`) is the accent-filled button directly under the camera in `CardDetail` and in an expanded `BrowsePane` row. It opens a per-bridge chat sheet: the posts since the last 4:00am Central reset (`chatDayStart`), oldest first, mine on the right in the accent color, others on the left. The thread refetches every 10 seconds while the sheet is open and sticks to the newest message unless the rider has scrolled up.

## Replies and notifications

A rider who is signed in can reply to someone else's message (the arrow under it). The composer then shows "Replying to {name}" with a cancel button, and the post goes out with `replyTo` (the parent's id). `check_in.reply_to` is a self reference with `on delete set null`, so deleting the original leaves the reply as a plain message. The server only accepts a parent that is live, not hidden, and at the same crossing; otherwise it posts a plain message. `listCheckIns` joins the parent and returns `replyTo` (first name, a `snippet` of the text, whether it had a photo); a hidden parent returns null. The reply shows that quote above its text.

`saveCheckIn` returns the parent's author, and `POST /api/check-in` then calls `notifyReply` (`lib/check-in-notify.server.ts`) inside `after()`, so the rider never waits on a push. It sends one Web Push to every device the author has registered: title is the replier's first name ("{name} replied to you", in the author's saved language), body is the bridge and the start of the reply, tag `chat-reply:{parent id}` so a newer reply replaces the old one, TTL one hour. Nothing is sent for a reply to your own post, to an author with no device, or past `REPLY_PUSHES_PER_HOUR` (10) from one sender to one author (`withinLimit`). The chat is closed overnight, and the POST is refused then, so no reply push goes out at night. A tap opens `/{port}?chat=1`; `CheckInButton` opens that chat once and removes the flag from the address.

How replying looks and feels: dragging someone else's bubble to the right replies (`SwipeToReply`, like Messages): it follows the finger with resistance, never goes left, an arrow grows in behind it, a tick marks `SWIPE_REPLY_AT` (44px of bubble travel), and letting go past that point (or a quick flick) starts the reply, otherwise it springs home. The reply arrow under the message does the same for a mouse or keyboard. The composer shows what is being answered as a rounded card above the box (accent bar, a thumbnail when the original had a photo, "Replying to {name}" in the accent color, the start of the text, and a round close button); it opens and closes in height with `SPRING.snap` (a fade with reduced motion). In the thread the quote is an inset card concentric with its bubble (bubble 24px, 8px inset, 16px card); tapping it scrolls to the original and gives that bubble a short scale nudge (`jumpTo`, no nudge with reduced motion), and does nothing when the original is gone.

A device only receives replies once it is registered. The bell at the top left of the sheet (shown while this browser has not been asked, or on an iPhone that is not on the Home Screen) calls `enablePush()` from the tap. A browser that already allowed notifications is re-registered with `resyncPush()` whenever the sheet opens, with no prompt. There is no separate off switch for replies: they follow the device's push address, which sign-out removes.

## Unread badges

A rider sees how many chat messages they have missed at their saved bridges, and when someone answered them. `chatSeenStore` (`lib/chat-seen-store.ts`, browser only) keeps, per bridge, the server time of the newest message the rider has seen; a bridge it has never tracked starts at "now", so old history is not a pile of unread. `useChatUnreadSync` (mounted once in `WalletApp`, saved bridges only, at most 12) asks `GET /api/check-in/unread?watch=port:sinceMs,...` once a minute while the tab is visible and writes one shared query (`["chat-unread"]`). `unreadCheckIns` answers in one query: posts by others, live, not hidden, not reported by this viewer, newer than `since` (and since the last reset), plus how many of those answer the viewer's own posts. Bridges with nothing new are left out. Guests get counts but no reply count. Overnight the client skips the call and the server answers `{ unread: {} }` without a query.

`useChatUnread(port)` only reads that shared query, so a card or button never fetches. `ChatUnreadPill` shows it: on the card face next to the place line (the part that stays visible in a stack), on the chat button (and on its closed-overnight row), whose second line also says "3 new · 1 reply to you". A reply to the rider's own message fills the pill in. The profile button carries a round total across all saved bridges (`useChatUnreadTotal`, signed in only), top right so it clears the streak badge at the bottom right. The open profile menu starts with an accent row, "New in the chat" and the same hint line, linking to `/{port}?chat=1` for the bridge that answered the rider (else the busiest). An open `CheckInSheet` calls `useMarkChatSeen` with the newest message on screen after every refresh, which moves the mark forward and clears that bridge's badge at once. No app icon badge: nothing runs while the app is closed to keep it right.

## Overnight

The chat is closed from 10:00pm to 4:00am Central, the same clock as threshold alerts (`chatClosed` in `lib/check-in.ts` wraps `thresholdQuiet`). `CheckInButton` swaps to a muted, untappable "Chat is closed" row (`useChatClosed`, rechecked each minute, so an open sheet unmounts when the hour strikes). The server enforces it before any session read or query: `GET /api/check-in` answers `{ checkIns: [], closed: true }`, and posting, reacting and reporting answer 403 `closed`. A closed chat does not touch the database, so it can suspend. The chat resets daily, not by deleting: `chatDayStart()` is the most recent 4:00am Central, and every read (thread, photo, reactions, reports, reply parents, unread) only sees posts made after it. The chat reopens empty at 4:00am; the old rows stay in the database.

## Who can do what

- Reading the thread and reacting: anyone, guests included. `GET /api/check-in` and `PUT /api/check-in/react` need no session.
- Posting and deleting your own post: signed in only (401 otherwise). A guest sees "Sign in to leave a message" and `SignInButton` in place of the composer.
- Reactions (`REACTIONS`: `like`, `helpful`) are one per rider per kind in `check_in_reaction`. `actor` is `u:<account id>` or `v:<visitor id>`. A guest's id comes from `visitorForActivity()` (the server-made visitor cookie, with its own limits), so a forged cookie cannot mint actors. Reads of a guest's own reactions only look at the cookie. Reactions are optimistic in the browser and refetched after.

## Location check

The rider's position is requested only on send, never on open. `checkPresence` (`lib/check-in.ts`, client-safe) compares the reading with `portLocation(port)`, the crossing's city point from `CITY_LOCATION`. The radius is `CHECK_IN_RADIUS_MILES` (a border town, not a parking lot, because a city's bridges can be miles apart), plus up to 1 km of the reading's own accuracy; worse than `MAX_ACCURACY_METERS` is refused as imprecise. The browser keeps the last good reading for `POSITION_REUSE_MS` so a conversation asks once, and runs the check first so someone plainly elsewhere hears it before uploading a photo. `POST /api/check-in` runs the same function against the port in `getBorderWaitTimes()` on every post, so the server never trusts the browser's verdict. A client can still lie about its coordinates; this keeps an honest rider honest, it is not proof. A city missing from `CITY_LOCATION` refuses with `unknown` instead of guessing.

## Storage

Coordinates are never saved. `check_in` (created on first use, cascades from `profile`) keeps `port_number`, `message`, `photo` (bytea), `miles_away`, and the time. A post needs a message or a photo. The photo is redrawn in the browser (`prepareJpeg`) to a JPEG of at most `PHOTO_MAX_EDGE` px and `MAX_CHECK_IN_PHOTO_BYTES`, which also strips camera metadata and EXIF location. The server accepts only JPEG magic bytes. `GET /api/check-in/photo/{id}` serves it while the post is live (`private` cache, `nosniff`); ids are random uuids.

Limits: `CHECK_IN_POSTS_PER_HOUR` posts per account (`withinLimit`), 200 reactions an hour per rider. Others see a first name only.

Sheet events are stopped at the portal root so a press inside it never reaches the card it was opened from. Escape closes the sheet first (capture, `preventDefault`), like the alert picker. Reduced motion swaps the slide for a fade.

A photo in the thread is a button that opens `PhotoViewer` full screen, inside the same portal at a higher layer. The picture follows a finger dragged up or down 1:1 while the backdrop thins, and closes past 110 px or on a flick; a tap on the backdrop, the close button, or Escape also closes it. Escape closes the viewer first, then the chat. Reduced motion turns the drag off and uses a fade.

Everything the rider does shows on screen before the server answers. A sent post is added to the thread at once, dimmed (`pending`), with the rider's own photo (`localPhoto`) so nothing is fetched back; the position read, upload and check run behind it, and a refusal removes it and returns the words to the box. Reactions and deletes change the thread in the query cache first. Requests go through one ordered queue, so rapid taps reach the server in order and the last one wins, and the 10-second refresh is held off while any are in flight so it cannot paint stale state over a tap. A reaction reads the thread as it is now, not the render the tap came from. `ref` and `localPhoto` are browser-only fields the refresh merges back in, so confirming a post never remounts its row. A failed request invalidates the thread, which rolls the screen back to the server's truth.

Deleting a post asks first (`ConfirmDelete`, an alert dialog over the chat). Cancel holds focus; the backdrop and Escape cancel, and Escape closes the confirmation before the photo viewer or the chat. Only Delete calls `remove`.

Language: `hasProfanity` (`lib/profanity.ts`, client-safe, English and Spanish) refuses a message that holds a listed word, whole words only, after undoing accents, leet characters (`sh1t`, `@ss`), stretched letters, and spaced-out letters. A few unambiguous slurs also match inside a longer token. The browser checks first so the rider hears it at once and keeps their text; `POST /api/check-in` repeats the check and answers 422 `language`. It is a first line, not a judge: it cannot read intent, so harassment in clean words still needs a report flow. Add a word to `WORDS` (no accents) and a passing case to `tests/profanity.test.ts` for anything it should not catch.

## Reports and retention

Anyone, guests included, can report another rider's post (`POST /api/check-in/report`, flag button, a reason from `REPORT_REASONS`; your own post cannot be reported). The reporter stops seeing it at once (the thread query leaves out posts that actor reported, and the browser removes it optimistically). When `REPORTS_TO_HIDE` different riders have reported it, `hidden_at` is set once and it disappears for everyone, its photo stops being served, and the author gets one `flagged` row in `usage_event`. An account with `HIDDEN_TO_MUTE` flagged posts inside `MUTE_WINDOW_HOURS` cannot post (403 `muted`); a failing moderation lookup lets the post through rather than blocking. Reports are only as honest as the riders: guests count by their visitor id (the server limits how many of those one address can make), and a report limit of 30 an hour applies per rider.

Posts are never deleted by a job; there is no check-in cron. A post stops being shown or served at the next 4:00am Central reset, but its row, photo, reactions and reports stay (only the author deleting it removes it). Also kept: every accepted post logs one `chat` row and every hidden post one `flagged` row in `usage_event`, per account, and the admin Feature use list totals both. Those rows hold no message text, photo, or place. Deleting your own post does not undo its `chat` count.
